    "case_summary": "A 31-year-old man presented with a painless tumor in the left inguinal region, which had gradually increased in size over the past six months. On physical examination, a subcutaneous tumor the size of a fist was noted in the inguinal area. The skin over the tumor appeared normal without any ulceration, and there was no enlargement of the inguinal lymph nodes. Computed tomography showed a solid tumor with heterogeneous contrast enhancement located in the adipose tissue, with no evidence of metastases to the liver or lungs. A wide local excision with a resection margin of at least 20 mm was performed. The excised tumor measured 124 × 105 mm, was solid, encapsulated, and had a homogeneous yellowish-white appearance without central necrosis or hemorrhage on its cut surface. Routine histological examination using hematoxylin-and-eosin staining revealed spindle-shaped cells with high cellularity in some areas and two mitoses per 10 high-power fields.\n\nThe tumor cells showed only immunoreactivity for desmin and alpha-smooth muscle actin.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
